id: pm-gh1465
title: "GH-1465: Represent implementer-to-verifier handoff as project context"
description: Assess reported lifecycle ambiguity when completed implementation is released for verification; distinguish a customizable project review queue from active work and preserve handoff evidence.
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-10-14T07:54:40.366Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-14T07:55:59.723Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: Reproduce claimed release warning and current status contracts; Evaluate existing custom status/workflow primitives before adding baseline states; Design explicit history-backed handoff and verifier context without marking inactive work in progress; Cover context selection and close/reopen semantics
parent: pm-dj98
risk: medium
confidence: medium
expected_result: Projects can express implementation-complete verification queues and accountable handoff without leaving inactive items in progress.
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-14T07:54:40.366Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: complete all-status corpus, fresh handoff searches and open/in-progress inventories; full canonical concurrency parent read. GH1465 body/all comments read. Report is intake, not independently reproduced; no exact existing owner found. Remains open/unclaimed."
body: "Source: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1465\n\n## Problem\n\nMulti-agent work commonly splits into **implementer → verifier**: an agent finishes, releases its claim, and an orchestrator (or reviewer) verifies the evidence before closing. pm has no lifecycle state for \"done, awaiting verification\". The allowed statuses are `draft, open, in_progress, blocked, closed, canceled`.\n\nSo every implementer ends with:\n\n```text\n$ pm release pm-dfrf\nstatus: \"in_progress\"\nwarnings[1]: released_unclaimed_in_progress\nsuggestions[1]: \"pm-dfrf remains in_progress and unclaimed. If work is paused, run pm release pm-dfrf --pause to move it to open.\"\n```\n\nThe warning is accurate, but in this workflow it is the intended outcome, not an anomaly. In one orchestration session (2026-10-14) two independent agents reported this warning as a probable pm bug. Neither available option fits:\n- `--pause` (back to `open`) loses the fact that the work is done.\n- `in_progress` + unclaimed reads as abandoned work. A verifier also cannot query its queue: `pm list --status in_progress` mixes active work with handed-off work.\n\n## Repro (2026.10.14)\n\n```bash\nd=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && git init -q && pm init >/dev/null\nid=$(pm create --type Task --title t --description d --acceptance-criteria a --json | jq -r .id)\npm claim \"$id\" && pm update \"$id\" --status in_progress && pm release \"$id\"\npm update \"$id\" --status in_review   # Invalid --status value. Allowed: draft, open, in_progress, blocked, closed, canceled\n```\n\n## Suggested change\n\nOne of the following, or both:\n1. A first-class review state, e.g. `in_review` (terminal-adjacent, not counted as active work), with `pm list --status in_review` as the verifier's queue. `pm close` from `in_review` keeps the existing close validation, and `pm item reopen`/`pm update --status in_progress` sends it back with a reason.\n2. `pm release <id> --handoff [--to <principal>] [--message <evidence summary>]`. It records the handoff (who, when, to whom, summary) in history, sets the review state (or a `handoff_to` field), and does not emit `released_unclaimed_in_progress`.\n\nContext management benefit: `pm context` could surface \"awaiting your verification\" separately from \"in progress\". That is exactly the split an orchestrator needs when 5–10 agents finish at different times."
